-- Finiteness WITNESSES for the lazy combinators (proposal library/003, WI-590).
--
-- These sorts carry no data. They are the CONDITIONAL INSTANCES that say
--
--     a mapped stream is finite WHEN ITS SOURCE IS
--
-- over the ONE lazy carrier in `combinators.anthill`, in place of a second,
-- structurally-identical FINITE twin carrier.
--
-- Before WI-590 this file held `FiniteMappedStream` / `FiniteFilteredStream`: full
-- carriers with their own entity, their own `collect` and their own `iterator`,
-- duplicating MappedStream / FilteredStream in everything but the source field's
-- type. `Iterable.map` built one, `FiniteCollection.map` the other, and a value
-- could not cross between them. What FORCED the duplication was ERASURE:
-- `mapped`'s source field was typed `Stream[Src, ES]`, so a mapped stream's type
-- did not record WHICH carrier the source was and no rule could condition on it.
-- WI-590 threads that sort as `MappedStream.Source`, which is exactly the handle a
-- witness needs: the `provides` below keys FiniteCollection on
-- `MappedStream[Source = S, …]` and the sort-level `requires FiniteCollection[C = S,
-- …]` GATES it on S's own finiteness. A mapped stream over a `List` provides
-- FiniteCollection; one over an infinite generator does not, so `collect` on it is
-- a load error rather than a diverging program. (EqList is the precedent: one
-- carrier, a witness sort supplying the conditional provision.)
--
-- Two consequences, and they are what shrank the file to a third of its size:
--
--   * NO `provides Iterable`. `FiniteCollection requires Iterable[C = C, …]`, and
--     the witness's carrier `MappedStream` already provides Iterable TRANSITIVELY
--     (`provides Stream`, and `Stream provides Iterable` — WI-492). The
--     provider-requires check reads the witness's carrier through its BASE SORT to
--     see that (one of the two typer fixes WI-590 landed first, pinned by
--     wi590_witness_param_carrier_test), so no `iterator` needs restating here.
--   * NO lazy `iterator` override. The old twins defined one only to route
--     `iterator` back onto the plain lazy carrier; that carrier IS the carrier now,
--     so its own `splitFirst` already serves.
--
-- `collect` (the finiteness PRIMITIVE) is the one thing a witness must supply, and
-- it stays EAGER: materialize the source, then map / filter it as a List. The inner
-- `collect(src)` is an abstract dispatch on `src : S` — a bare sort param, whose
-- `Element`/`E` are grounded by the sort-level `requires FiniteCollection[C = S, …]`
-- (the WI-590 typer capability `bind_spec_params_from_enclosing_requires`; without
-- it the row leaks and the op reports an undeclared effect). It must be SORT-level,
-- not on the op: an override may not strengthen a spec-op precondition (WI-347).
sort anthill.prelude.MappedStreamFinite
  import anthill.prelude.{FiniteCollection, MappedStream, List, Function}
  import anthill.prelude.FiniteCollection.{collect}
  import anthill.prelude.MappedStream.{mapped}
  import anthill.prelude.List.{mapElems}

  sort S = ?                             -- the SOURCE carrier — what finiteness is keyed on
  sort Src = ?                           -- source element
  sort T = ?                             -- mapped element (fn's result)
  -- WI-441 decoupled rows (cf. MappedStream): source access row + transform row;
  -- the provided collection's row is their UNION (both paid on consumption).
  effects ES = ?
  effects EF = ?

  requires FiniteCollection[C = S, Element = Src, E = ES]

  -- THE WITNESS — read it as the conditional instance it is: GIVEN the `requires`
  -- above, a MappedStream whose Source is S provides FiniteCollection.
  provides FiniteCollection[C = MappedStream[Source = S, Src = Src, T = T, ES = ES, EF = EF], Element = T, E = {ES, EF}]

  -- EAGER: materialize the source, then map `fn` over the resulting List.
  -- `collect(src)` pays the source's access row ES; `mapElems(…, fn)` pays EF.
  operation collect(m: MappedStream[Source = S, Src = Src, T = T, ES = ES, EF = EF])
    -> List[T = T] effects {ES, EF} =
    match m
      case mapped(src, fn) -> mapElems(collect(src), fn)
end

-- The filter witness. A kept element is returned VERBATIM, so the source element
-- type IS the result element type (`T` on both sides) — cf. FilteredStream.
sort anthill.prelude.FilteredStreamFinite
  import anthill.prelude.{FiniteCollection, FilteredStream, List, Function, Bool}
  import anthill.prelude.FiniteCollection.{collect}
  import anthill.prelude.FilteredStream.{filtered}
  import anthill.prelude.List.{filterElems}

  sort S = ?                             -- the SOURCE carrier
  sort T = ?                             -- element (kept verbatim, so src = result)
  effects ES = ?
  effects EF = ?

  requires FiniteCollection[C = S, Element = T, E = ES]

  provides FiniteCollection[C = FilteredStream[Source = S, T = T, ES = ES, EF = EF], Element = T, E = {ES, EF}]

  operation collect(f: FilteredStream[Source = S, T = T, ES = ES, EF = EF])
    -> List[T = T] effects {ES, EF} =
    match f
      case filtered(src, pred) -> filterElems(collect(src), pred)
end
